rvival  of the liberal democrats at stake here  well i think that's possible and i think  actually  we need revived liberalism in this  country which is the  the core part of my pitch you know it's  now been three general elections in a  row  we're bumping along at six percent in  the polls  change has never been more necessary in  fact it's essential  for the survival of our party and what i  want to do as leaders to reverse that  decline  and it starts by actually having a bold  progressive  liberal agenda that we can show the  country and actually coronavirus  as awful as the circumstances are  actually  shows us that there should be and could  be another way  in this country has exposed the  inequalities that we see before our very  eyes  and the civil liberties of the  individual the freedom of us  all um has come under some strain  now is the time for a revived liberal  democrat party and that's very much  central to my pitch  okay um i'm trying to uh answer the  question i guess a lot of people  probably have which is you know  what are the lib dems about what's the  point of them what do they stand for  now you suggested last month that you  wanted to move the lib benz to the left  you said  i wanted to be seen as more radical than  labour the manifesto you're publishing  today calls for things like a universal  basic income but then earlier this month  you said  i don't want us to be on the left of  labour so i'm a bit confused  do you want the lib dems to be a more  left-wing party or not  i want us to be more radical so when i  said more radical than labor that was  not left  it was radical liberalism and i think  that's what i found  interesting about the conversations  around this is that actually that left  right axis  doesn't work when you think about  liberalism on the one hand  we have a strong commitment to public  services because you need as an  individual the security  to live life as you choose and that's  why we talk about you know changes to  universal credit having a welfare system  that stops people from falling through  the cracks but on the other hand  we are a friend to business i also want  the party to make sure that  we are on the side of business on the  side of entrepreneurs  in my manifesto that's released today i  talk about  looking at the economy and making it  work particularly for small business  that's not left that's not right that's  liberal and core  to my uh three pillars of the campaign  and when i when i came on in uh  on your show just a few months ago i  spoke about making us the party of  education again  talking about the environment these are  two very important things but the third  pillar that i've added since then  is around the economy we have huge  challenges that our country is going to  face over the next months and years  and this is the opportunity to make sure  that the economy isn't just measured in  terms of growth and gdp  but that we also put other indicators  around it like mental health and  well-being so there is another way  of having our country thrive and it's a  liberal way not left not right  okay now it hasn't been the smoothest  start to your leadership campaign  uh that you could have wished for you  had to delete a tweet which mimicked her  style was  character that some people thought was  racist he deleted a potentially libelous  claim against the housing secretary  robert gemerick  figures you collated about children  admitted to hospital with malnutrition  turned out to be wrong  do you think some people might be  wondering if you've really got the  judgment to be a lib dem leader  well there are no perfect campaigns  mistakes have been made on both sides  in this leadership election but what we  are also finding is that core message  is bringing people towards us and what i  would say though  is we have to win back trust with the  voters you know this is the big thing  that we need to now  do and we also need to show them that we  are  credible over the last few weeks i've  delivered the coronavirus compensation  scheme  cross party you know this is a scheme  that  helps bereaved families of nhs frontline  social care  and nhs workers we need to show people  that the point of voting libdem right  now  is to have someone who can get stuff  done  for them and their family in a way  that's really palpable i've shown that i  have  the ability to do that i've shown that i  can get cut through in the media  to get that message across at a time  when people are wondering where the lib  dems have gone  this is a really critical moment for our  party and unless we change and unless we  move forward together  then i'm seriously concerned about the  future of the party and that's why i'm  encouraging people to vote for me  the momentum in this campaign is very  much with me  and the last internal poll had me  slightly ahead
 

